{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head f235ad071bdebdde14cd93631173f7d44aeee4d7 (tree 2468416ee6db2df2daaea96cec15c81741b1a39b), the E5 design-note continuation on my approval c5c9bc61 at 4f801add. The verdict binds the E5 implementation promise adf6f94b and signs the one changed note artifact, 9ac96a71. My earlier verdict c5c9bc61 stays the approval of the other 16 paths: `git diff --name-only 4f801add f235ad07` lists only `notes/2026-11-14-act-environment.md`, so those 16 blobs are identical at both heads.\n\n## The four ordered edits\nPlanner act 0aa78756 ordered four edits. All four are present and read true against the code I reviewed at 4f801add.\n1. **Status wrap.** The paragraph is rewrapped. Its facts are unchanged. \"E5 added sequential dependencies between authorizations\" is true, and \"Section 19 lists later rulings, including E5\" is true because the f364097e row sits in the section 19 table. The sentence on section 18 still names only the E1 rulings, which section 18 holds.\n2. **Section 7.4.** The paragraph states what E5 built: a checked hypothetical prefix under E2 standing selects through a pending source. When it cannot establish the selector, the submit stops with `source` before retention and signing and writes nothing. A recorded source takes the ordinary path. In `scope-client.ts` the first retention step throws `PendingVia` before any write, `#prepareVia` throws `source` when the chain is not admitted, and `retainAuthorizations` commits nothing on that path. The deleted sentence \"The first version has no call that builds one\" is gone with reason: a private composition path now builds the graph.\n3. **Section 8.1.** \"Checked Explore supplies each step's key and version... E5 uses these sources in its private composition path. Public composition waits for `ActEnvironment`.\" This matches ruling f364097e and `registerComposition`, which the package entry does not export.\n4. **Rulings row.** The f364097e row carries the three answer